{"text": "Journal of Chomaroeropy, $98 (1992) 3465-380\nEnevier Soence Polishers BY.. Amsterdam\n\nCHRON. 25964\n\nShort Communication\n\nDetermination of maleic hydrazide in tobacco by micellar\nliquid chromatography\n\nS.S. Yang\nResearch Center, Philp Merris CSA, P.O. Bos 28583 Ricnond, VA 28361-6583 USA)\n\n(irs: received November 10h, 1981; reise manuscript recived Deserber 18h. 1991)\n\nABSTRACT\n\nA reversed-phase high performance liquid chromatographic method with UV detection was developed o determine maleic hydride\n{MH residues in rohacc. Sample preparation conssed of an intial hydrolysis of MH residue mith 12 AY hydhochlorc ach by\nsonication at an elevated temperature. followed by. neutalzaion ith an aqurous sodium hydroxide solton, Chromatogrephic\nSeparation was performed on s Cy, column with 4 mabile phase containing 40 mM etsitimethstarmmoniam brome in 0 8\n\n\u2018hosp buer(pH = 70), Other Sample prepuraton procedures were inesigated alu\n\nSiren ener sources,\n\n\u2018ltacton digestion with be ai of\n\nconventional eater and micronate oven! and these results ae also cused.\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\nMaleic hydrazide (MH) is a. synthetic plant\ngrowth regulator widely used in tobacco farming as\n1 suckering control agent. The normal practice is 10\napply MH to the upper haf of tobacco plants short-\nly after topping. The applied MH then gradually\ntranslocates to other parts ofthe plant. The fate and\n\u2018metabolism of MH in tobacco plants has been in-\nvestigated using foliar-absorbed '*C-labelled MH\n(0). This study showed that MH and its metabolites\ntranslocated to actively growing tissues ofthe whole\nplant and some of these compounds are extractable\n\u2018with methanol. The major component in the metha-\nnol exiract was identified asa f-b-glucoside of MH.\nA review article published in 1987 [2] discussed in\n\u2018detail the absorption, translocation and metabolism\nof MH in tobacco\nA number of analytical procedures for the deter-\nrmination of MH residues in tobacco or tobacco\nproducts have been published. The distillation\n\n021.9673 92 S000. \u00a2\n\nspectrophotometric method and its various. mod:\nifications [3-6] have been widely used. In these pro-\ncedures MH residues are reductively hydrolyzed to\nhydrazine by zine in a concentrated alkali solution.\n\u2018The evolved hydrazine is subsequently derivatized\nwith p-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde, and measured\nspectrophotometrically at 455:nm, A gas chromato-\n\u2018graphic method consisting of hydrolysis of MH re-\ns in 2 M hydrochloric acid at 90-95\u00b0C and de-\nization with N,O-bis(trimethylsilyacetamide\nhas also been reported [7]. In addition. an immu-\nrnoassay method using a monoclonal antibody-\nbased enzyme for the analysis of MH was described\nin 1989 (8)\n\u2018We describe here a reversed-phase high-perform-\nance liquid chromatographic (HPLC) procedure for\nthe determination of MH residues in tobacco and\ntobacco products. The sample preparation involves.\nhydrolysis of MH residues in 12 M hydrochloric\nacid, followed by neutralization with a sodium hy-\n\u2018droxide solution after cooling the sample in a cold\n\n1992 Elsevier Science Publishers BV. All ight reserved\n\n2021201113",
